David Ploog wrote:

> ...I learned a huge amount
> about design from Nethack. I cannot say I admire it anymore, though: the
> design seems too erratic in many places. For example, lots of little
> details added in the game are made useless by other features. 

ie, the game was balanced but then somebody had an idea they thought 
was so nifty they had to put it in.... Unihorn healing, alchemy, 
etc. are cruft (and somewhat detrimental cruft) added to the simpler, 
better, more balanced game that nethack contains. 

I am a fan of the idea that there should be more than one way to do 
something.  But there are several things which I think are too powerful 
and ought to be nerfed, or at least made much more difficult to obtain.


> Cannot say anything about the code. But I know that Crawl was written
> because the original authour wanted to avoid some deficits of Angband and
> Nethack. (In Nethack, some of these are: permanent resistances from
> eating; identical ascension kits; broken features like Elbereth and
> genocide.)

Yeah.  Identical ascension kits bother me too, although it's only 
one symptom of the problem that I focus on.  That problem I
call "thousanduplet syndrome" and it consists of winning characters 
all looking too much alike, as though some poor woman somewhere had 
given birth to identical thousanduplets  (and a complicated and 
painful labor it must have been, since all the kids are armored and 
have large pointy things with them). By endgame, virtually all
characters have the same set of capabilities, or very near to it.  
This is much worse in Angband than it is in Nethack, but it definitely 
is there. Nethack's artifact limit does help to avoid this, BTW, but 
people who are then allowed to wish for the artifact of their choice 
(and most nethack characters do) tend to wind up similarly kitted.
Crawl IMO does a much better job of avoiding thousanduplet syndrome, 
largely because of the design of its skill set. 
 
> Nethack certainly has strengths: the starting pet (and their behaviour)
> is one, the shops are another. When I was a Nethack player, I thought that
> the extreme care about minute details (TDTTOE) is awesome, but I am not
> convinced anymore. In some sense, this approach paved the way to the
> current "spoiler & win" status and it also killed a part of the game
> inside (for me).

The extreme care about minute details is awe-inspiring, but it is also 
a bit too predictable (and often too much to the player's advantage) to 
be always good.  I have to say I admire it, just because of the sheer 
work it took, and I think it's the kind of thing that's important for 
a game's codebase (or anyway one targeted at this niche, which mine is) to
support.  But yes, it needs to be part of game design that's there for 
a reason, and right now it mostly seems like it was added because someone
thought it was cool.  Perhaps better design would make the outcomes more
random and ambiguous. 
 
> Good luck! Are you working on a grand epic, or could you release a smaller
> but playable version?

I will definitely release a simple, playable, winnable game first.  :-)
But the codebase under it will be ready to support development of a
grand epic, and once the simple game is out there the grand epic will 
be along pretty rapidly  (although early versions will be longer on 
content than balance, inevitably; balancing requires feedback).
 
> If you are interested, I reckon that any modern roguelike needs to come
> with Tiles (alongside to ASCII) and with a tutorial. It is good to keep
> server play in mind (even if you don't care about in your first released
> version, the architecture should allow adding it later).

I'm not doing tiles at this point, because personally I don't prefer 
them.  But I am targeting server play and wide-curses for Unicode 
terminals.  Tiles, maybe later.  Or maybe falcons-eye or similar 
can be adapted.  
 
> I think that a game should challenge its
> master players, too, but that's a different design principle.

It's a much more difficult design principle to achieve, particularly
when the code is publically released and people can make arbitrarily
precise spoilers.  I agree that it's the ideal, but I'm not holding 
out for it because bluntly I think it's just plain too hard to do. 
 
